fact WorkItem(id: "WI-734", description: some(value: "WI-714 follow-up (cleanups from the join review): (1) Concat abstract-operand NEUTRAL behavior. Concat[A, B] is a bare type CONSTRUCTOR (sort.anthill, no entities, inert like Type) reduced by the internal typer op reduce_concat_in_type at the return-type normalization boundary. Its behavior when an operand is ABSTRACT (a generic wrapper whose schema is not yet known -- e.g. a Relation param inside a generic op, not a concrete named-tuple) is undecided: today it is a loud error in non-arg position. Decide whether an unreduced Concat over an abstract operand should stay SYMBOLIC (reduce later, once the operand grounds -- the neutral/lazy reading, which would let generic wrappers over join type-check) or remain a loud error. The same question applies to Without[T, Drop] (WI-727, the dual) and to any Project[T, keep] (WI-732), so settle it ONCE for the family rather than per-constructor. (2) relation_ref_arg_type -> stamp cleanup. relation_ref_arg_type (kb/typing.rs) computes a bare RULE-ref argument's Relation[T] ON DEMAND so a sibling callback param can project it at hint time (join's r2: varref_arg_env_type only covered a stamped receiver). That is a recompute where a STAMP would do -- align it with how the receiver path already stamps inferred_type, so a bare rule-ref arg and a bare rule-ref receiver share ONE mechanism instead of two. Both are review follow-ups from the join increment (6185dbc2), neither a correctness bug: /code-review (5 agents) found no correctness defect in join. ACCEPTANCE: Concat/Without/(Project) share one settled, documented rule for abstract operands, with a test pinning it in each direction; relation_ref_arg_type's on-demand recompute is retired in favor of the stamp (or a comment records why it must stay); existing wi714_join / wi727_fix tests green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T15:15:30Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-734", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. DECISION: residual-on-abstract, loud-on-concrete, settled once for the family. An operand that is NOT YET KNOWN leaves the ctor SYMBOLIC; an operand that IS known but unmergeable stays LOUD. \"Not yet known\" = a logic variable (ViewHead::Var -- MEASURED: a generic op's [S] reaches the reducer as Var::Rigid) OR an unreduced residual one level down. Deliberately NOT keyed on TypeExtractor::Error: that is a total-ness catch-all covering malformed types too, so keying on it would silently convert real errors into residuals. Locus: operand_not_yet_known + a guard arm in reduce_binary_type_ctor (kb/typing.rs). TWO TICKET PREMISES WERE FALSE (measured, not reasoned): (1) \"which would let generic wrappers over join type-check\" is WRONG -- a generic op calling join with abstract schemas fails EARLIER at row-lambda column resolution ('type mismatch in <unresolved receiver>.name ... no such member (dot dispatch)'), so Concat is never reached; and a wrapper that FORWARDS the lambda is blocked differently ('type mismatch in conjoin_of.cond (op-arg): expected NodeOccurrence') because the WI-722 macro needs the lambda syntactically. Generic join wrappers stay blocked either way -- this change does not buy them. The live case is `fix` (no lambda): `wrapFix[S](p: Relation[T = S]) -> Relation = p.fix(name: \"a\")`. (2) An abstract operand was never a DECLARATION-time problem: an op whose signature writes Concat[A = L, B = R] over its own type params ALREADY loaded clean, because reduction fires only at CALL sites. VERIFIED END-TO-END (this was the load-bearing claim, so it was measured, not argued): a residual CONFORMS in a generic wrapper, and REDUCES once its operands ground -- a concrete call to the wrapper resolves a column from EACH operand (m.(name, dept)), which only the merged schema admits. Negative control: an unknown column is still rejected after grounding. ESCAPE HAZARD CHECKED: widening a wrapper to a bare `-> Relation` lets a residual escape unreduced (the gate reads the DECLARED return). A/B-verified this is SAFE, not a silent wrong schema -- a column use on an escaped residual, dropped or kept alike, fails LOUDLY at dot dispatch, because an unreduced ctor offers no named-tuple schema. Widening costs usability, never correctness. PART 2 -- relation_ref_arg_type MUST STAY, and the reason is ORDERING, not oversight (the acceptance's \"or a comment records why it must stay\" branch). A/B-verified: dropping the rung from projection_receiver_type fails all five wi714_join tests with '<unresolved receiver>.name/.who/.dept'. Its consumer is the row-lambda's callback param HINT; at the DotApply frame only the RECEIVER is pre-typed (WI-443), so when join(r1, lambda (c,q) -> ..) hints q with r2's schema, r2 has not been typed yet and carries NO stamp. A stamp cannot satisfy a reader that runs before the producer; retiring it would require pre-typing sibling args, which is what WI-443 removed. Rationale recorded on the fn. EXTRA (from /code-review, serves WI-732 + WI-759 directly): the family was enumerated in TWO independent places (return_reducible_ctors' hardcoded (bool,bool), the reduction boundary's if-chain) and the first draft added a THIRD. Now ONE const BINARY_TYPE_CTORS drives both -- the gate returns a positional [bool; N] and the boundary FOLDS over the family -- so adding Project[T,keep] or FieldOf is one line instead of three edits where missing one silently disables reduction. Also hoisted the ctor-symbol resolution out of the per-operand path (try_resolve_symbol is string-keyed; the gate exists precisely to keep that off the common path). TESTS: 7 in wi734_abstract_operand_test.rs -- Concat abstract-symbolic, residual-reduces-when-grounded, unknown-column negative control, Concat concrete-collision-loud, NESTED residual (the second not-yet-known shape, which a bare-variable test cannot reach), DENOTED operand still loud (a value can never be a named tuple = cannot-reduce-EVER, pinning that the rule did not over-broaden), Without abstract-symbolic via the real fix path. Full workspace: 3038 passed, 0 failed. Docs updated on both stdlib ctors (sort.anthill) + reduce_binary_type_ctor. LEFT OPEN (judgment call, not in acceptance): the residual escape via a widened return is documented but not DIAGNOSED -- the user learns of it at a far-away use site rather than at the widening. A warning at the widening point would name the actual cause; CLAUDE.md's loud-over-silent principle arguably wants one.", at: "2026-07-18T15:15:28Z")
